\section{Input data}\label{sec:data}

%#python
import pandas as pd

# Geometry (made up)
span = 9.0            # m, between supports
width = 2.5           # m, usable deck width

# Materials: a CSV table in the project's resources. The path is relative to
# the main document's folder («1. Main»).
mat = pd.read_csv("../999. Resources/1. Data/materials.csv", index_col="material")
steel = mat.loc["S275"]

# Loads: an Excel sheet (read just as in Jupyter).
loads = pd.read_excel("../999. Resources/1. Data/loads.xlsx", index_col="load")
q_perm = loads.loc[["Self-weight", "Timber deck", "Railings"], "value"].sum()
q_imp = loads.loc["Imposed load", "value"]
%#end

\subsection{Geometry}
The footbridge spans \py{span}~m with a usable width of \py{width}~m.
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor.

\subsection{Materials}
The values of table~\ref{tab:materials} are read from the file
\texttt{materials.csv}. Each cell of the table is a \texttt{\textbackslash py\{\}}
that asks the pandas table for one value: \texttt{mat.loc["S275", "fy"]}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Materials (read from \texttt{materials.csv}).}
  \label{tab:materials}
  \begin{tabular}{lccc}
    \toprule
    Material & $f_y$ (MPa) & $E$ (GPa) & $\gamma_M$ \\
    \midrule
    Steel S235 & \py{mat.loc["S235", "fy"]} & \py{mat.loc["S235", "E"]} & \py{mat.loc["S235", "gamma_M"]} \\
    Steel S275 & \py{mat.loc["S275", "fy"]} & \py{mat.loc["S275", "E"]} & \py{mat.loc["S275", "gamma_M"]} \\
    Steel S355 & \py{mat.loc["S355", "fy"]} & \py{mat.loc["S355", "E"]} & \py{mat.loc["S355", "gamma_M"]} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Steel \textbf{S275} is used, with a yield strength of \py{steel["fy"]}~MPa.

\subsection{Actions}
The loads per metre of beam are in the sheet \texttt{loads.xlsx}. This time the
whole table is written by Python with \texttt{tabletex} (table~\ref{tab:loads}).

\py{tabletex(loads.reset_index(), caption="Loads per metre of beam (read from \\texttt{loads.xlsx}).", label="tab:loads", prec=2)}

They add up to \py{f"{q_perm:.2f}"}~kN/m of permanent loads and \py{f"{q_imp:.2f}"}~kN/m of
imposed load.
